# OAuth2: Authorization Code + PKCE Flow
# User authentication (opens browser for login, captures callback locally)
# PKCE (Proof Key for Code Exchange) is automatically handled

@baseUrl = https://api.example.com
@oauth2-cache = .tokens.json

###

### Authorization Code Flow with PKCE (browser popup)
# When you run this request:
# 1. A browser window opens to the authorization URL
# 2. You log in and authorize the application
# 3. You're redirected to a local callback server
# 4. The code is captured and exchanged for a token
# 5. Token is cached and injected as: Authorization: Bearer <token>

# @oauth2-grant = authorization_code
# @oauth2-token-url = https://auth.example.com/oauth/token
# @oauth2-auth-url = https://auth.example.com/oauth/authorize
# @oauth2-client-id = {{CLIENT_ID}}
# @oauth2-scope = openid profile email
GET {{baseUrl}}/user/profile

###

### With custom redirect port
# The callback server listens on localhost:9876 by default
# You can specify a different port with @oauth2-redirect-port
# Make sure this port is registered in your OAuth2 app as a redirect URI

# @oauth2-grant = authorization_code
# @oauth2-token-url = https://auth.example.com/oauth/token
# @oauth2-auth-url = https://auth.example.com/oauth/authorize
# @oauth2-client-id = my-client-id
# @oauth2-scope = read write
# @oauth2-redirect-port = 8080
GET {{baseUrl}}/protected

###

### Multiple scopes
# Scopes are space-separated

# @oauth2-grant = authorization_code
# @oauth2-token-url = https://auth.example.com/oauth/token
# @oauth2-auth-url = https://auth.example.com/oauth/authorize
# @oauth2-client-id = {{CLIENT_ID}}
# @oauth2-scope = openid profile email offline_access
GET {{baseUrl}}/user/preferences

###

### POST request with Authorization Code
# @oauth2-grant = authorization_code
# @oauth2-token-url = https://auth.example.com/oauth/token
# @oauth2-auth-url = https://auth.example.com/oauth/authorize
# @oauth2-client-id = my-client
# @oauth2-scope = read write
POST {{baseUrl}}/user/update
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "New Name",
  "email": "newemail@example.com"
}

###

### Using environment variables
# @oauth2-grant = authorization_code
# @oauth2-token-url = {{$env OAUTH2_TOKEN_URL}}
# @oauth2-auth-url = {{$env OAUTH2_AUTH_URL}}
# @oauth2-client-id = {{$env OAUTH2_CLIENT_ID}}
# @oauth2-scope = read write
GET {{baseUrl}}/data

###

### Token reuse and refresh
# Tokens are cached in .tokens.json with:
# - Cache key: grant::tokenURL::clientID::scope
# - When you run a second request with the same parameters,
#   the cached token is reused (if not expired)
# - Tokens are checked for expiry with a 30-second buffer
#   (refreshes 30 seconds before actual expiry)

# First request - browser opens, user logs in
# @oauth2-grant = authorization_code
# @oauth2-token-url = https://auth.example.com/oauth/token
# @oauth2-auth-url = https://auth.example.com/oauth/authorize
# @oauth2-client-id = my-client
# @oauth2-scope = openid profile
GET {{baseUrl}}/data/1

###

# Second request - uses cached token (no browser popup)
# @oauth2-grant = authorization_code
# @oauth2-token-url = https://auth.example.com/oauth/token
# @oauth2-auth-url = https://auth.example.com/oauth/authorize
# @oauth2-client-id = my-client
# @oauth2-scope = openid profile
GET {{baseUrl}}/data/2

###

### PKCE details (automatic, you don't need to do anything)
# PKCE (RFC 7636) is automatically implemented:
# - Code challenge is generated from a random code verifier
# - Challenge is sent to authorization endpoint
# - Verifier is sent during token exchange
# - Protects against authorization code interception attacks
# - Enabled by default, no configuration needed
